<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eucratides I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 170–145 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Eucratides the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eucratides I was a Greco-Bactrian king who seized power around 170 BCE, overthrowing the Euthydemid dynasty and fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> kings south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>. He issued some of the finest Hellenistic coins, including the largest gold coin of antiquity, a twenty-stater piece. He was murdered around 145 BCE, reportedly by his own son, and soon afterwards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> fell to nomad invaders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eucratides-i.jpg" alt="Eucratides I" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eucratides I / BnF · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eucratides_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
